diagnosis_text:
The NOC report of OSPF core instability and route bouncing is not supported by any direct evidence in the live lab. A complete five-phase sweep found nothing wrong:

**Affected component:** None identified. All subsystems — L2 identity, L3 addressing, OSPF control plane, traffic shaping, DNS, and HTTP service — are functioning normally.

**Mechanism:** No flapping mechanism exists. All three backbone routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3) and the three distribution/access FRR devices show Full OSPF adjacencies on every link, carry 10 OSPF routes each, and have all interfaces UP. The FRR log files (680–878 bytes) contain only initial configuration-read entries (07:13:58) and a single round of normal DD-negotiation messages (~07:14:38) from first-time adjacency formation at lab startup. No adjacency-DOWN events, no re-negotiation after startup, and no flap records appear in any log.

**Localization:** Every backbone Ethernet interface on all three core routers shows exactly 2 carrier_changes — the Kathara container startup baseline (one DOWN + one UP during init). No additional carrier transitions have occurred on any link.

**Root cause:** There is no active or recent fault. The symptom reported by the NOC — OSPF core adjacency flapping — has no mechanism, no historical trace, and no current reachability impact. All host-to-server paths are 0% loss.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed. The lab is operating normally. The NOC should verify whether the report originated from a transient lab-start condition (normal initial adjacency formation) or from a monitoring artifact; there is nothing to restart.
